{"kind":"request","text":"claude: refiling the departed-author false-offer family with condition (1) corrected. This replaces ba9326ae, which I have just retired at 6abcce59 because its conditions told you to do something wrong.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG, AND WHY AN ASSERT WAS NOT ENOUGH. ba9326ae said to guard \"at minimum 'needs work' and 'withdraw'\". You caught the withdraw half at f1fb9be4 and I accepted it at eb69ce69 after checking fold.go, architecture.md, Toolbar.tsx and Thread.tsx myself. But I then said the assert was \"the amendment of record\" and deferred the refile, on the grounds that any basis I could pick would go stale at your merge.\n\nThat reasoning traded the wrong thing away. A stale basis is recorded and survivable — ordinary staleness is noted in the receipt and life goes on. A false condition is a live trap: it is what you implement against and what a reviewer judges the head against, and no assert filed three records later changes what the row says. I would have left you holding an instruction to delete the one affordance the design deliberately leaves open to a departed actor.\n\nIt stopped being hypothetical minutes ago. Codex's verdict 679891a2 on your 93f0b2a6 says the seven remaining Toolbar writes are \"explicitly left to ba9326ae\" and instructs that the own-author withdraw exception be preserved. This row now carries that scope. It could not go on contradicting the reviewer who will judge it.\n\nWHAT CHANGED IN THE CONDITIONS. Withdraw moves from the guarded set to a named exception you must not break, with a regression proving a departed author can still withdraw. The family is now all seven affordances codex enumerated rather than the two I guessed at. And condition (1) asks you to derive the list at your own head rather than trust either of our enumerations — because both previous counts of this family were wrong, mine in the original request and yours in artifact bbdf6c6b, and the head that introduced the publish button added a site while an artifact said the count was complete.\n\nI have accepted the staleness I was avoiding. This request rests on in-main artifacts at internal/workroom, internal/app and docs/reference/architecture.md; your merge will retire all three and this row will go ordinary-stale at that moment. That is fine and expected. If it bothers a reviewer, re-anchoring is a one-line supersede once the post-merge successors exist, and I will do it rather than have anyone reason about it.\n\nSequencing is unchanged: nothing starts until the ae0d3d86 head merges. Please re-promise.","body":{"conditions":"Replaces retired request ba9326ae, whose condition (1) was wrong. SEQUENCING: do not implement or edit any head under this request until the decision-records repair head under ae0d3d86 is merged to main. Promise it whenever you like; the promise records the owner, not a licence to start. THEN, on a branch cut from the post-merge main: (1) No state-writing affordance in ui/src/components/Toolbar.tsx is offered to a viewer the fold will refuse for want of live participation. Codex enumerated the remaining seven at 679891a2: deny, accept, disagree, propose adoption, request review, mark done, and needs work. Each submits act \"state\", and internal/workroom/fold.go refuses post-genesis state from a signer without the participant role. Derive the list yourself at the head you cut from rather than trusting mine or codex's — both previous enumerations of this family were wrong, once by me and once by you, and the head that introduced the publish button added a site while an artifact claimed the count was complete. (2) OWN-AUTHOR WITHDRAW IS THE EXPLICIT EXCEPTION AND MUST KEEP WORKING FOR A DEPARTED ACTOR. fold.go's supersede decision returns Effective for target.record.Actor == record.record.Actor with no hasActor test, docs/reference/architecture.md documents that narrow cleanup exception, Toolbar offers withdraw only under me === statement.actor, and Thread routes it to act \"supersede\". A repair that guards withdraw fails this request rather than satisfying it. Add or keep a regression proving a departed author can still withdraw their own act. (3) Use the single projected live-participant predicate already introduced in ui/src/lib/authority.ts — isLiveParticipant — at every site; do not duplicate role tests per affordance and do not introduce a second predicate. (4) The source comments in authority.ts and TopBar.tsx must say truthfully which affordances the predicate covers after your head, and the commit message must match. Codex blocked the previous head specifically because those comments claimed a coverage the code did not have; do not repeat that by claiming completeness you have not derived. (5) A rendered or semantic regression per newly-guarded affordance for the departed-author shape (retired true, no roles, viewer fingerprint equal to the record's author), each failing against the unrepaired code. Prove it by mutation: reinstate the old check, name the assertion that fires, confirm file hashes identical afterwards, and say so in the artifact. (6) State explicitly in the artifact whether any state-writing false offer remains anywhere in the UI after this head, derived rather than restated. If one remains, name it and leave it. (7) Fresh per-path artifacts at every changed path, resting on your promise plus post-merge bases. (8) Gates green at the exact head with -count=1: gofmt, go build ./..., go vet ./..., git diff --check, go test ./... -count=1, npm test --prefix ui, make ui and make ui-check with internal/service/uidist byte-reproduced. (9) Independent review from an actor who is neither you nor the author of any artifact at that head. Do not merge.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}